# ibnIPS backend configuration
#
# Copy to .env and fill in:
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# The server reads these from the OS environment first, then from a .env file
# in the working directory. .env supports # comments and quoted values.
#
# .env is gitignored. Never commit real values.

# --- Required -------------------------------------------------------------

# Secret for signing JWTs. Minimum 32 characters, no placeholder values —
# the server refuses to start otherwise, because a key that exists in this
# repository would let anyone mint a valid token for any user.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
JWT_SECRET=

# Shared access key that clients send to POST /api/auth. Minimum 16 chars.
# This is what stops anyone claiming another user's account.
#   openssl rand -hex 16
AUTH_KEY=

# --- Database -------------------------------------------------------------

# Path to the SQLite file. IGNORED when DATABASE_URL/DATABASE_TOKEN below are
# set to real values — those take precedence, and the server will say so at boot.
#
# Local: leave as icps.db, and leave the Turso keys empty or commented out.
# On Render set this to /tmp/icps.db, because the working directory is read-only
# there.
#
# WARNING: /tmp is wiped on every redeploy. Fingerprints collected since the
# last deploy are lost. A persistent disk is the fix — mount one and point
# DB_PATH at it, e.g. DB_PATH=/data/icps.db
DB_PATH=icps.db

# --- Optional -------------------------------------------------------------

# Listen port. Defaults to 3000.
# PORT=3000

# Remote URL for the map served by GET /api/map. Falls back to a local
# map.json when unset. map.json is gitignored, so set this in production.
# MAP_JSON_URL=

# --- Turso ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The backend DOES read these, and they win over DB_PATH. When both are set to
# real values the server talks to Turso instead of opening the local file.
#
# DANGER: this file is loaded automatically from the working directory, so
# filling these in here means every local `gleam run` writes to that remote
# database — including throwaway test data, and including your real production
# one. The server prints which database it opened at startup; check that line
# before you type anything into it.
#
# Prefer keeping production credentials in Render's dashboard only, and leaving
# these blank locally. To work against Turso on purpose, set them as real
# environment variables for that one command rather than committing them here.
#
# Find both in the Turso dashboard for your database.
# DATABASE_URL=
# DATABASE_TOKEN=